      "The weight is spread fairly evenly across three look\u2011alikes: 10 Oct 2022, 28 Jul 2025 and 17 Jun 2024. The weeks after them were mixed: two saw slight falls in cancellations, one (in the summer holidays) a clear rise. For GP reports, the main external drivers are the working year and school holidays (staff and managers away slow activity and can change cancellation patterns), with a secondary seasonal effect from autumn illness disrupting attendance. The forecast week, 02 Nov 2026, is a full, term\u2011time week just after October half term, whereas one key look\u2011alike sits deep in summer holidays and another is pre\u2011half\u2011term rather than after. Only the June look\u2011alike resembles a normal term week. Overall this looks like a match in the numbers more than in shared context.",
      "The forecast points to a clear fall on the latest week and the last quarter, and a slight fall on the last month. For an early\u2011November, post\u2011half\u2011term week that feels plausible: activity typically normalises after the break, which can steady GP report bookings and cancellations, though rising seasonal illness could still nudge cancellations up. In practice, free cancellations and income at risk should dip below recent levels rather than surge. One thing to watch is any backlog catch\u2011up after half term, which could tighten slots and reduce cancellations less than forecast."
